---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const b = appBySlug('shopify-email')!;
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify only send newsletters?",
    "answer": "No. Shopify documents marketing automations and abandoned-checkout recovery in Shopify Messaging, the current documentation home for Shopify Email."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Klaviyo worth evaluating?",
    "answer": "When a named event, segment or branching requirement remains unmet after checking the native workflow. Prove that requirement before changing senders."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both checkout flows run?",
    "answer": "Choose one checkout owner and reconcile pending sends before enabling its replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the basket examples measured results?",
    "answer": "No. They are fixed-goods-cost gross-profit calculations, excluding other operating expenses."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Find the native recovery gap before paying to rebuild it.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Start with", "tool": "Shopify", "reason": "Check the existing automation and its delivery history."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Klaviyo", "reason": "Prove the event or branching requirement that native recovery cannot meet."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Start with a missing behavior, not a product ranking</h2>
<p>For a merchant whose main requirement is an email back to an unfinished checkout, inspect the Shopify automation first. A move to Klaviyo should solve a specific problem: an earlier cart-stage audience, a segment-specific path, or recovery coordinated with an existing Klaviyo lifecycle program. Write down the failed case and the staff time it consumes. A different dashboard is not evidence of additional recovered orders.</p>
<h2>Shopify Email has a recovery workflow</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify’s current documentation uses Shopify Messaging</a> and includes abandonment and welcome automations. Its abandoned-checkout emails are excluded from the email send count. Do not apply a blanket monthly-email allowance to checkout recovery costs or describe the native product as newsletter-only. Check other campaign and channel charges separately in the account.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">Shopify’s migration guide</a> says the checkout automation defaults to email marketing subscribers and exposes delivery details on the automation page. A missing email therefore calls for inspecting eligibility and the delivery record before replacing the app. Compare subscriber and non-subscriber cases using the actual configured recipient policy.</p>
<h2>Separate earlier cart intent from checkout recovery</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/28709780787355">Klaviyo documents automatic Shopify Added to Cart tracking</a> through onsite tracking, including a migration from the older metric. Audit existing metric references before creating a new earlier-stage flow. A cart reminder must stop when the customer advances to checkout; a second metric should not become a second independent stream of offers.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Klaviyo’s Shopify data reference</a> explains that Checkout Started includes eventual buyers, so a recovery flow needs a delay and purchase exclusion. It also instructs merchants replacing Shopify recovery to disable the Shopify messages manually. Neither installing an integration nor receiving a checkout event proves that suppression is correct. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: prove the native gap</h2>
<p>Use an identified customer who adds an item but never starts checkout, then another who supplies an email at checkout. Record which event exists, who is eligible, the planned send and the return destination. Purchase during the delay and inspect the remaining queue. Repeat with a new browser and a returning subscriber. These are proposed tests, not observed vendor results. Keep the native workflow if it covers the required cases at acceptable operating cost.</p>
<h2>Repair checkout friction before escalating channels</h2>
<p>For a bundle with an unapplied offer, verify discount eligibility and combinations in the destination checkout. For an international basket, explain shipping and duties instead of promising that a code removes them. A BNPL or financing-widget exit needs an accurate payment explanation, not assumed credit approval. For B2B below MOQ, link to the minimum or a smaller pack. None of those content repairs requires a new email platform by itself.</p>
<p>On mobile, open the return link outside the original browser and check the selected size, quantity, gift message and subscription choice. Remove an out-of-stock variant and inspect the message again; do not promote an unavailable basket. Returning buyers should receive relevant assistance rather than a new-customer offer. Treat refunds as a separate service decision rather than restarting abandonment. Where a required field is absent from the event, repair the mapping or use neutral copy.</p>
<h2>Put a price on the discount before the platform</h2>
<p>Hypothetical $432 basket at 50% gross margin: fixed goods cost is $216, full-price gross profit $216, and a 20% discount leaves $129.60, a loss of $86.40 per order. A $687 basket at 49% margin has $350.37 goods cost and $336.63 full-price gross profit; a 17% discount leaves $219.84, a $116.79 reduction. These are not recommended caps and exclude shipping, fees, returns, labor and platform costs.</p>
<p>Compare incremental gross profit against the native baseline, then subtract incremental software and operating cost. Assign eligible shoppers to a stable control and treatment, keep the measurement window equal, and count orders once in the store ledger. Choose sample size from actual volume and the effect worth detecting; a universal 5% holdout may be uninformative. Vendor-attributed recovery alone does not establish lift.</p>
<h2>Change the owner without overlapping queues</h2>
<p>Inventory legacy checkout notifications, Shopify automations, Klaviyo flows and any SMS sender. For each, record its trigger, eligibility, purchase exit, pending queue and owner. Pause new enrollment in the old checkout flow, decide whether pending messages drain or are canceled, and enable the replacement only after purchase and unsubscribe checks pass. Keep order confirmations separately owned. If a pilot fails, pause new enrollment and reconcile its pending sends before restoring the previous workflow.</p>
<p>Earlier cart, checkout and welcome flows need a shared offer policy even if they have different owners. If SMS is added, verify channel consent, local quiet hours and cross-channel frequency limits with the actual sender; the original arbitrary timing ladder is not a performance finding. Keep a ledger of sends and skipped decisions so a support agent can explain why one shopper received two messages or none.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
